      "path": "e2e/d8r/names.spec.ts",
      "content": "This substitutes raw DOM text for the browser's computed accessible name, so the equality check can now pass when a screen reader omits the contract. For example, `\u003cbutton\u003e\u003cspan aria-label=\"Open\"\u003eXYZ Dec 18 $55 Call\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/button\u003e` has the accessible name \"Open\", but this code assigns the button the contract text and discards the span's text because it is not `byContent`. The page-level snapshot check also passes because it only validates contract mentions that exist. Retain browser-computed names for visible elements (or use a standards-compliant accessible-name implementation), and handle hidden elements separately rather than replacing accessible-name computation with `textContent`.",
      "existing_code": "      const accessibleName = squash(targets.map((found) =\u003e found.textContent).join(' ')) || element.getAttribute('aria-label') || element.getAttribute('alt')\n        || element.getAttribute('title') || (named ? text : '');",
      "start_line": 44,
      "end_line": 45,
      "category": "test",
      "severity": "medium"
    },
    {
      "path": "e2e/d8r/names.spec.ts",
      "content": "Restricting `text` to `byContent` elements skips the visible-text/name comparison for explicitly labelled elements with other roles. For example, `\u003cdiv role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Open\"\u003eXYZ Dec 18 $55 Call\u003c/div\u003e` is now recorded with `text: ''`, so all assertions pass even though its accessible name omits the visible contract. The previous leaf-text scan included this case. Preserve the visible text for explicitly labelled elements as well, rather than using the name-from-content role list to decide whether to check it.",
      "existing_code": "      return accessibleName ? [{ accessibleName, text: named ? text : '' }] : [];",
